<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Easter</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pascha, Pasch, Resurrection Sunday</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Easter, also called Pascha or Resurrection Sunday, is a Christian celebration and cultural holiday commemorating the resurrection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> describes as taking place on the third day after his crucifixion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at Calvary. It is preceded by Lent, a 40-day period of fasting, prayer and penance, and by Holy Week, and opens Eastertide, which lasts until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentecost">Pentecost</a>. A moveable feast, its date was set by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a> (325) as the first Sunday after the first full moon on or after the vernal equinox. It is linked to the Jewish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a> by its name, its origin and much of its symbolism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/easter.jpg" alt="Easter" width="330" height="373" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Piero della Francesca · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Resurrection.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Every <b>Easter</b> Monday the abbess of San Zaccaria presented the doge with a new linen cap made by her nuns.</blockquote><a href="/societies/venice">Social Hierarchy of the Republic of Venice</a></li><li><blockquote>Later stanzas take up <b>Easter</b>, the Passion and a litany of the saints.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bogurodzica">Bogurodzica (Mother of God)</a></li><li><blockquote>Fred Kaan's Lent and <b>Easter</b> hymn &quot;We Have a King Who Rides a Donkey&quot; is sung to the same tune.</blockquote><a href="/songs/drunken-sailor">Drunken Sailor</a></li><li><blockquote>Priests were later allowed to sing it, first only at <b>Easter</b> and on the day of their ordination; by the end of the 11th century they used it on Sundays and feasts outside Lent.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gloria-in-excelsis-deo">Gloria in excelsis Deo</a></li><li><blockquote>Wesley had meant the words to be sung to the same tune as his <b>Easter</b> hymn &quot;Christ the Lord Is Risen Today&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hark-the-herald-angels-sing">Hark! The Herald Angels Sing</a></li><li><blockquote>On <b>Easter</b> Sunday, 9 April 1939, the African American contralto Marian Anderson sang it at the Lincoln Memorial: she had been barred from singing at the DAR Constitution Hall under its whites-only policy, and after a national outcry, and with the support of Eleanor Roosevelt, the concert was…</blockquote><a href="/songs/my-country-tis-of-thee">My Country, 'Tis of Thee</a></li></ul></section>
